Thus the issue: ArrowRight keydown handler stops propagation and prevents default, then finds next active element and calls show() on its Tab instance. But perhaps the focus is not being set because after show(), the tab button does not receive focus. In Bootstrap tab JS, show() activates the tab but does not focus it. Usually you'd need to focus the tab after showing. The spec expects that arrow navigation moves focus to the next tab and selects it.

In the observed after HTML, the notifications button has class "active" and aria-selected=true, but it does not have tabindex=0 (it has tabindex="-1"? Actually after HTML shows the notifications button: `<button class=\"nav-link active\" id=\"tab-notifications\" ... aria-selected=\"true\">Notifications</button>` It lacks tabindex attribute (so default is 0? Actually button elements are focusable by default, tabindex not needed. But maybe because it's inside a listitem with role presentation? Still button is focusable. Yet focus didn't go there. Possibly because the keydown handler prevented default and stopped propagation, but then called show() which may cause blur? Or focus remains on previous element because show() doesn't focus.

Looking at the tab.js _keydown method:

```
_keydown(event) {
    if (!([ARROW_LEFT_KEY, ARROW_RIGHT_KEY, ARROW_UP_KEY, ARROW_DOWN_KEY].includes(event.key))) {
      return
    }

    event.stopPropagation()// stopPropagation/preventDefault both added to support up/down keys without scrolling the page
    event.preventDefault()
    const isNext = [ARROW_RIGHT_KEY, ARROW_DOWN_KEY].includes(event.key)
    const nextActiveElement = getNextActiveElement(this._getChildren().filter(element => !isDisabled(element)), event.target, isNext, true)

    if (nextActiveElement) {
      Tab.getOrCreateInstance(nextActiveElement).show()
    }
  }
```

It prevents default and stops propagation, then finds next active element (excluding disabled) and calls show() on that element's Tab instance.

But show() does not focus the element. In Bootstrap tab, showing a tab does not manage focus. Usually you'd want to focus the tab after showing. The ARIA authoring practices guide for tablist says that arrow keys should move focus to the next/previous tab and set aria-selected accordingly.

Thus we need to modify _keydown to also focus the nextActiveElement after showing (or instead of show? Actually show already activates tab and updates aria-selected etc. We just need to move focus to the tab.

So we can add: nextActiveElement.focus() after calling show().

Alternatively, we could modify show() to focus, but better to keep minimal edit in tab.js _keydown.

